 ## Operating characteristics
 *   **Time Complexity:** Rendering is efficient due to limited sampling: $N_s=6$ planes for background and $N_d=7$ points per dynamic object intersection [S1].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Depends on the number of objects and the size of the MLPs. Latent codes are stored per object [S1].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Depends on the number of objects and the size of the MLPs, with latent codes stored per object [S1].
 *   **Performance:** Optimization takes approximately one full day (350k iterations) on a single NVIDIA TITAN Xp GPU [S1].
-*   **Strengths:** Handles dynamic scenes with temporal consistency; separates static and dynamic elements; shares weights by object class; handles novel view synthesis well [S1].
-*   **Limitations:** Requires 3D tracking data for object poses and bounding boxes; not limited to automotive datasets but primarily evaluated on KITTI/VKITTI [S1].
+*   **Strengths:** Handles dynamic scenes with temporal consistency, separates static and dynamic elements, shares weights by object class, and enables novel view synthesis [S1].
+*   **Limitations:** Requires 3D tracking data for object poses and bounding boxes, and is primarily evaluated on automotive datasets like KITTI and Virtual KITTI 2 [S1].
 *   **When to use:** Dynamic scene reconstruction and novel view synthesis where objects move rigidly and can be tracked [S1].
 
 ## Background
-Neural Scene Graphs were introduced in 2021 by the authors of the paper "Neural Scene Graphs for Dynamic Scenes" [S1]. The method was developed to address limitations in existing neural rendering techniques (like NeRF) when applied to dynamic scenes, specifically ghosting and lack of temporal consistency. It builds upon the concept of Neural Graph Mapping by defining a specific DAG structure for dynamic environments [S1, Parent]. It has been used on automotive datasets like KITTI and Virtual KITTI 2 [S1].
